# unix-socket-open  (generated by harness/probes.py)
Q: can a shell write to an AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM socket by redirection?

A live listening socket exists at /tmp/li-phase0-a/live.sock.

bash  redirection_result="printf 'probe\\n' > /tmp/li-phase0-a/live.sock; echo rc=$?\nbash: /tmp/li-phase0-a/live.sock"  daemon_received=False
zsh   redirection_result="printf 'probe\\n' > /tmp/li-phase0-a/live.sock; echo rc=$?\nzsh: no such device or address: "  daemon_received=False
fish  redirection_result="⟦LI-P⟧ printf ⟦LI-P⟧ printf 'probe\\n' ⟦LI-P⟧ printf 'probe\\n' >⟦LI-P⟧ printf 'probe\\n' > ⟦"  daemon_received=False
zsh   zsocket attempt='module_path=(<WORKSPACE>/experiments/toolchain/root/usr/lib/zsh/5.9.2 $module_path); zmodload zsh/net/s'

CONCLUSION: paths that are sockets cannot be opened with > : bash reports
ENXIO (no such device or address), zsh and fish are no better. A shell
hook therefore cannot dial the daemon; the FD must be inherited from the
launcher, or an external helper must do the connect.
zsh/net/socket is the only exception and it is an extra module that the
unpacked zsh copy must load explicitly.
